# Summary
Introduced: ListObjectVersions now collects per-PG results without a hard cap, and the max-keys input is unbounded, allowing attacker-controlled memory growth and database work.
The commit wires ListObjectVersions to query each placement group and then aggregates all results into a single in-memory vector. Unlike ListObjectsV2, this path does not enforce the global MAX_LIST_RECORDS cap. The HTTP layer also parses max-keys directly into a u32 without an upper bound. An authenticated client can pass an extremely large max-keys value (e.g., near u32::MAX), causing each PG to return a large number of records that are then accumulated and sorted. With sufficiently large buckets, this can exhaust memory and crash the server, resulting in a denial of service.

# Reviewer note
Reviewed against commit `195d31f` and the current tree behavior. This is a valid medium-severity availability issue.

- At commit `195d31f`, `ListObjectVersions` parses `max-keys` directly into `u32` with no upper bound in [crates/server/src/http/mod.rs](/home/justin/src/github.com/justincormack/argmin/crates/server/src/http/mod.rs), then forwards it unchanged into the coordinator.
- The same commit's coordinator path in [crates/server/src/coordinator.rs](/home/justin/src/github.com/justincormack/argmin/crates/server/src/coordinator.rs) fans out to every PG, requests `max_keys.saturating_add(1)` from each, and extends a single `Vec` with no `MAX_LIST_RECORDS` guard, unlike the neighboring `ListObjectsV2` implementation.
- The storage path in [crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs](/home/justin/src/github.com/justincormack/argmin/crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs) translates that into `LIMIT max_keys + 1`, so a large authenticated request amplifies both database work and in-process memory growth.
- The current tree still has the same bug shape under renamed crates: `parse_max_keys` only validates syntax, `Coordinator::list_object_versions` still aggregates without a global cap, and the targeted tests only cover malformed `max-keys`, not large-but-valid values.

Focused validation run locally:

- `cargo test -p server-http list_object_versions_invalid_max_keys -- --nocapture`
- `cargo test -p server-core list_object_versions_with_sparse_pg_topology -- --nocapture`

Both passed, but neither exercises the large `max-keys` path.

One caveat on the generated patch: adding a hard cap is necessary, but truncating the unsorted cross-PG accumulator before global ordering is proven can change pagination order and markers. Any fix should preserve AWS-compatible ordering while bounding memory.

