## Report
Build attempts to enable crash/valgrind/debugger validation failed due to network-restricted dependency fetches: `cargo build` could not download tiny_http from crates.io (403), and `cargo build --offline` reported no matching package, leaving no binary to run. After this failure, I validated via code review. The HTTP handler for ListObjectVersions parses `max-keys` directly into a u32 with no upper bound and forwards it unchanged to the coordinator (crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:258-275). The coordinator’s list_object_versions fan-outs across all placement groups, requests `max_keys.saturating_add(1)` per PG, and extends a single `all_versions` Vec with no global cap (crates/server/src/coordinator.rs:1226-1238). In contrast, ListObjectsV2 enforces a hard `MAX_LIST_RECORDS` cap and truncates the global vector (crates/server/src/coordinator.rs:1090-1104; constant at 22-24). The storage layer uses SQL LIMIT `max_keys+1` per PG (crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:593-598), so very large max-keys forces large DB work and large per-PG result sets. This confirms the reported unbounded memory growth path for authenticated clients sending large max-keys on ListObjectVersions, enabling DoS via memory exhaustion.

